instructions:
1) Edit packages/inbox/wrangler.jsonc: set DEFAULT_FROM to an address on your email domain (e.g. inbox@mail.example.com).
2) Create two instance files in packages/infra/environments/<env>/:

   // inbox.ts
   import { cloudflareModule } from '../../modules/cloudflare'
   export default cloudflareModule.instance({
     name: 'inbox',
     config: { workdir: 'packages/inbox', accountId: '<account id>', workerSecrets: ['INBOX_TOKEN'] },
   })

   // email.ts
   import { cloudflareEmailModule } from '../../modules/cloudflare-email'
   import inbox from './inbox'
   export default cloudflareEmailModule.instance({
     name: 'email',
     imports: [inbox],
     config: {
       accountId: '<account id>', zoneId: '<zone id>', domain: 'mail.<your-domain>',
       enableSending: true, enableRouting: true,
       catchAll: { action: 'worker', workerName: { from: 'inbox', output: 'workerName' } },
     },
   })

3) Add INBOX_TOKEN (any long random string, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) to secrets.yaml, and make sure CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN has the scopes the cloudflare-email module documents.
4) `zbc apply <env>`. Create the R2 bucket if wrangler asks for it (`wrangler r2 bucket create <project>-inbox-raw`).
Note: Cloudflare holds newly onboarded sending domains in a preview sandbox (verified recipients only) for a short period after DNS validates; arbitrary-recipient sending starts working once it drops.
